Fixes every leg applies (build_turnip.sh, build_turnip_perf.sh, build_turnip_wayland.sh,
build_turnip_linux.sh) through apply_common.sh, before the variant's own EXTRA_PATCH/EXTRA_SCRIPT.
They are KGSL-backend bugs, so Android builds carry them too.

DROPPED 2026-10-08: both KGSL patches this directory used to carry are now in Mesa main,
part of MR44838 (Danylo Piliaiev, Igalia), verified present at our pin 3172302832:

  1da50a1b940  tu/kgsl: Correctly merge timestamps and sync files  (2026-10-01)
    Replaces kgsl-syncobj-merge-ts-fd.patch (DroidDeck, 2026-09-26). Ours fixed the NULL
    dereference by converting the timestamp side to a fence before merging; upstream
    rewrote kgsl_syncobj_merge outright, converting ret via kgsl_syncobj_ts_to_fd(&ret)
    exactly as we did, and additionally propagates fd errors instead of asserting. Strictly
    a superset of our fix, so the patch was deleted rather than rebased - it no longer
    applied at all, since the function it patched is gone.

  e984ef294ea  tu/kgsl: Prevent indefinite wait times for 0 timeout waits  (2026-09-30)
    Replaces kgsl-zero-timeout-poll.patch (The412Banner, 2026-09-29; upstream credits him
    by name). Same mechanism: a zero/already-past timeout reads
    IOCTL_KGSL_CMDSTREAM_READTIMESTAMP_CTXTID with KGSL_TIMESTAMP_RETIRED and answers
    VK_SUCCESS/VK_TIMEOUT instead of reaching the wait ioctl that KGSL reads as "forever".
    Upstream's kgsl_poll_timestamp takes struct tu_device *; ours took a raw int fd.

    This one is worth recording because it did not fail loudly. After upstream changed
    wait_timestamp_safe's signature, our patch still "applied" at --fuzz 3 and inserted
    kgsl_timestamp_retired(fd, ...) into a function whose parameter is now `device` - a
    compile error, shadowing the identical branch upstream had already added. apply_common.sh
    asserted on a string the fuzzed result did contain, so the gate passed on code that
    could not build. A green build is not evidence a patch is correct.

  Consequence: the A6xx/A7xx series is now empty and this layer no longer guards the A740.
  That is correct - Mesa carries both fixes - but do not read apply_common.sh as a
  correctness gate for that driver any more. The probes below still assert tu_mesh.cc does
  not reach a non-A8xx build, which is the one thing this layer still checks.

A8XX DRIVER ONLY (since 2026-09-30, after r7): ../a8xx-winnative/0001-0006 are applied by
apply_common.sh only when EXTRA_PATCH is the gen8 stack. 0001 (mesh) and 0002 (wave32) also switch
on for A7xx (0002 for A6xx gen4 too: has_getfiberid), where they can steer DX12 games onto
emulated paths (compute mesh + forced sysmem, half-wave subgroups); a user felt A7xx slower on
r6/r7. The A6xx/A7xx drivers therefore carry none of it, and apply_common.sh fails if
tu_mesh.cc reaches a non-A8xx driver.

a8xx-winnative/0001-0006 (Max / MaxsTechReview, WinNative-Emu/Drivers linux/patches, GPL-3.0;
  0001-0005 2026-09-27/28, 0006 c71f600a 2026-09-29). Kept under Max's own names and numbers so
  they can be diffed against his repo; they build on each other and apply in that order, after
  kgsl-syncobj-merge-ts-fd and before kgsl-zero-timeout-poll (both since dropped as upstream, see
  above). Replaces our earlier renamed copies of
  0003-0005 (a8xx-*.patch, 2026-09-28), which had 0004 adapted because 0001 was missing.
  - 0001 mesh shaders: VK_EXT_mesh_shader emulated with compute. Task/mesh shaders write into a
    device ring that a generated vertex shader draws; render passes with mesh draws use sysmem.
    Exposed on chip >= A7XX with >= 32 KiB compute shared memory.
    REBASED onto Mesa 4da08cb (2026-09-30): upstream folded tu_shader_serialize/deserialize into
    tu_shader_cache_process_blob(), so Max's two MESA_SHADER_COMPUTE blob hunks are one
    io.bytes(&shader->mesh) case there. At fuzz 3 the old hunks landed outside any function.
    Everything else is Max's diff unchanged.
  - 0002 wave32: a required subgroup size of 32 on A8XX's 64-wide waves (each half-wave is a
    subgroup), so vkd3d-proton accepts WaveSize(32) shaders.
  - 0003 cube-coord sanitize: a NaN/zero cube-map direction hangs A8XX; clamp NaN to 0 and turn the
    zero vector into +Z (quirk on a8xx_base only).
  - 0004 bindless invalidate: A8XX's SP_UPDATE_CNTL has no bindless bits; invalidate through
    SP_GFX/CS_BINDLESS_INVALIDATE. Older chips unchanged.
  - 0005 IB VBO alias: freeing command-stream memory with GPUMEM_FREE_ID hangs A8XX; IBs are fetched
    through a KGSL VBO alias that is unbound before the free (A8XX only).
  - 0006 IB cache: reuse retired A8XX IB BOs + aliases instead of unbind/free churn during play
    (per-device, 256 MiB idle cap, freed at device destroy). TU_KGSL_IB_CACHE=false disables it.
    Max: RedMagic A840v2 passed movement/menus/exit in FF7 Rebirth; long play + warm relaunch
    still unverified on his side.
  All six verified 2026-09-30 on Mesa 4da08cb for every leg's recipe (Standard, A8xx + gen8 stack,
  710-720, 8G2 One UI; Android, Wayland pre-steps, Linux pre-patches): zero rejects, zero fuzz.
  When Mesa moves and one stops applying, rebase it here (or pull Max's rebase) - never drop it
  silently.

Dropped-patch measurement record, kept because it explains what e984ef294ea fixes and what
  it was worth on our hardware (kgsl-zero-timeout-poll.patch, The412Banner, 2026-09-29):
  wait_timestamp_safe() turned a poll (a timeout that is zero or already past) into
  IOCTL_KGSL_DEVICE_WAITTIMESTAMP_CTXTID with timeout 0, which KGSL treats as "wait
  forever". Mesa's emulated timeline semaphores poll from vk_sync_timeline_gc_locked on
  every submit that signals a timeline, while holding the timeline mutex, so vkd3d-proton
  (a timeline signal per submit plus a fence thread on the same mutex) ran CPU and GPU in
  lock-step. Found on Bannerlator's Wayland adapter (Pocket FIT A750): a D3D12 demo capped
  at ~615 fps went to 4335 fps with the same answer given from outside the driver; X11 D3D12
  (809 fps) had the same stall, milder. See docs/KGSL_ZERO_TIMEOUT_POLL.md.
